      "message": "Thanks, Will!\n\u003e 1\n\nAgreed. I moved this patch after aosp/4368056, so `vm_insert_page()` already maps every slice here. Nothing else in b9/b10 touches `mm/vmalloc.c`, so the move is clean.\n\n\u003e 2\n\nOnly a direct `remap_vmalloc_range_partial()` call on a sub-range can over-map. It isn\u0027t exported, its only caller (vmcore) isn\u0027t built in GKI, and vmcore\u0027s chunks are whole kernel pages, so with `vm_slice_off \u003d\u003d 0` nothing outside a chunk gets mapped. Checking this did turn up a related bug: with an interior `kaddr`, the slice offset could take the range past the end of the area. The range is now bounded from `area-\u003eaddr` (the same check as in Kelvin\u0027s aosp/4367949), so that case fails with `-EINVAL`.\n\n\u003e 3\n\nA non-zero `vm_slice_off` needs an `mmap()` offset that isn\u0027t 16KB-aligned. Driver cookies (vb2, xsk, rxe, siw) are kernel-page multiples, and native processes can\u0027t pass such an offset, so `-EINVAL` is the right result. Starting at slice 0 instead would give PTEs that don\u0027t match `vma_slice_offset()`, which `__access_remote_vm()`, GUP and futex keys rely on. Cookie drivers can refuse a slice in `-\u003emmap()`, as Kelvin\u0027s aosp/4367609 does for vb2.\n\nOn the squash, on the `remap_vmalloc_range()` path, aosp/4367949 maps the same PTEs with the same bound and adds the slice the same way, so it doesn\u0027t change (3). It also needs `vm_insert_page_slice()` from aosp/4367599, i.e. driver-selected slices, so I\u0027d rather leave both in Kelvin\u0027s series.",
